# 🧩 The Mathematician's Chessboard Puzzle

Two prisoners, Alice and Bob, are offered a chance to escape by solving a puzzle.

The king places 64 coins on a chessboard, one on each square. Every coin shows either heads or tails, arranged however the king chooses. (It is 2^64) &#197;

The king then secretly hides a diamond beneath one of the 64 squares. Anywhere he wants.;

### The Rules

1. **Alice enters first.  She can see the entire board and knows exactly where the diamond is hidden.
2. Alice must flip exactly one coin (heads to tails or tails to heads). She cannot move or rearrange any coins.
3. Alice leaves, and Bob enters. He can see the final arrangement of all 64 coins, but he does not know where the diamond is or which coin Alice flipped.
4. Alice and Bob cannot communicate during the experiment, but they may agree on a strategy beforehand.
5. Neither prisoner knows the initial arrangement of the coins in advance.

### The Challenge

**Can Alice and Bob devise a strategy that guarantees Bob will identify the exact square containing the diamond?**

The strategy must work for every possible initial arrangement of the 64 coins and every possible diamond location.

If you believe it is possible, describe the strategy and prove why it always works.
